    CUDA HC: the wide up read, one warp per row, opt-in (STRATA_GR_UP_WIDE=1)
    
    The up projection of the hyper-connection read runs 160 blocks of 8 warps
    (each warp takes 8 rows) on a 12.0 card, so 160 blocks of 192 SMs and ~6.7
    warps an SM: the read is latency-bound at about 0.5 TB/s.  This kernel takes
    one row per warp and 2 columns x the 4 streams a block, so the grid is
    N/2 = 1280 blocks (53 warps an SM).  Every row's dot, xor tree, epilogue and
    the cross-stream sum are gr_up_multi_kernel's, in its order, so the outputs
    are bitwise the same; the token streams of both arms were identical in all
    6 pairs measured.
    
    Measured on l620 (RTX PRO 5000 72 GB, all experts in VRAM, IQ3_S, 3
    interleaved pairs, fresh engine each): story 197.4 -> 201.3 tok/s (+1.9%),
    code 243.0 -> 244.7 (+0.7%).  Off by default (STRATA_GR_UP_WIDE unset); the
    q8_1 tail of S26 needs the 16-column blocks, so the wide kernel is only used
    when no q8_1 image is written.

